The serializer plumber2 ships as \code{html} only understands a bare string or a
single \link[htmltools:tags]{htmltools::tags} element. A \code{tagList} — what \code{\link[=hx_table_rows]{hx_table_rows()}} and
most fragment helpers return — matches neither, so it falls through to
plumber2's XML branch and is sent as unusable markup, with a \code{200} status
and no error. Reaching for \verb{@serializer none} sidesteps that, but labels the
response \code{text/plain}.

\code{htmx} handles both, and always sets \code{text/html}:
\itemize{
\item \code{tagList} and single tags (as returned by \code{\link[=hx_page]{hx_page()}}, \code{\link[=hx_table_rows]{hx_table_rows()}})
\item strings marked with \code{\link[htmltools:HTML]{htmltools::HTML()}}
\item plain character vectors, concatenated as-is
\item \code{NULL}, sent as an empty body — useful for a swap that clears its target
}

Anything else is an error rather than malformed markup. Content that is not
htmltools output — an SVG device string, JSON — belongs on the matching
plumber2 serializer instead.

The serializer is registered when htmxr is loaded, so \code{library(htmxr)} at
the top of your API file is enough to make \verb{@serializer htmx} resolve.
